  <section|Mathematical scope and inputs><label|mathematical-scope-and-inputs>

  This work responds to the request for the entire existence argument and
  remains in progress. The manuscript now proves finite Wilson foundations,
  scalar rate control, conditional flat-step transport, ordered extraction,
  all-mesh smooth convergence from explicit finite-difference/Cauchy inputs,
  reflection-positive dynamics, ground-algebra disintegration, conditional
  phase selection and regularity, null-retention criteria, complex heat time,
  joint tempered boundary values, mixed heat-vector Gram limits, and
  common-domain field reconstruction. The subsequent chapters prove Fourier
  support, Lorentz covariance, full-tube continuation, locality, field
  detection and joint momentum positivity from the displayed earlier inputs,
  following the main Lean steps. These proofs discharge former reconstruction
  interface assumptions but do not yet establish the actual RG, calibration,
  ultraviolet or OPE/trace estimates. The final conditional assembly is not
  the complete existence proof.

  The shared <hlink|glossary|../../glossary.tm> and <hlink|notation
  protocol|../../../docs/notation.tm> determine the main conventions.
  <verbatim|G> is the fixed closed subgroup of <verbatim|U(r_G)>;
  <verbatim|P_p> is the physical period, <verbatim|D_N> the denominator,
  <verbatim|a_N=D_N^{-1}>, and the site count per axis is <verbatim|P_p D_N>.
  The finite link action is <verbatim|S_ell>, and continuum correlations use
  <verbatim|S> with insertion/phase indices. The extraction section declares
  its own correlation notation and generic domains. The reflection section
  uses an inner product conjugate-linear in its first argument. The selected
  phase is distinct from the mixed hierarchy throughout.

  The existing <hlink|YM-000022|../YM-000022/correspondence.tm> proves
  running-coefficient asymptotic extraction from an explicit running
  estimate. This article does not duplicate that proof or assert that the
  underlying running estimate has been established by either unit.

